n# • INFO • BIB • PDF • ARXIV •\n\nA proof of purely singular splitting conjecture\n\nA set $M$ of nonzero integers is said to split a finite abelian group $G$ if there exists a subset $S\\subseteq G$ such that $M\\cdot S = G\\setminus\\{0\\}$. Such a splitting is called purely singular if every prime divisor of $|G|$ divides some element of $M$. In 1995, Woldar \\cite{W1995} conjectured that the finite abelian groups admitting a purely singular splitting by the set $\\{1,2,\\dots,k\\}$ are precisely the cyclic groups of orders $1$, $k+1$, and $2k+1$. In this paper, we prove this conjecture.\n\ncyclic group finite abelian group group\n\n2025-05-13\n\nKa Hin Leung, Ran Tao, Daohua Wang, Tao Zhang\n\n# • INFO • BIB • PDF • ARXIV •\n\nOn lattice tilings of $\\mathbb{Z}^n$ by limited magnitude error balls $\\mathcal{B}(n,2,k_{1},k_{2})$ with $k_1>k_2$\n\nLattice tilings of $\\mathbb{Z}^n$ by limited-magnitude error balls correspond to linear perfect codes under such error models and play a crucial role in flash memory applications. In this work, we establish three main results. First, we fully determine the existence of lattice tilings by $\\mathcal{B}(n,2,3,0)$ in all dimensions $n$. Second, we completely resolve the case $k_1=k_2+1$. Finally, we prove that for any integers $k_1>k_2\\ge0$ where $k_1+k_2+1$ is composite, no lattice tiling of $\\mathbb{Z}^n$ by the error ball $\\mathcal{B}(n,2,k_1,k_2)$ exists for sufficiently large $n$.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nUnion-Closed Sets Conjecture Survey | PDF | Vertex (Graph Theory) | Theoretical Computer Science (https://www.scribd.com/document/808206070/UCSurvey)\nciteturn6search2 [wordlim: 200] Published: 13.0 years ago; Crawled: 1.9 years ago; These observations are due to Poonen, who also found exceptions to them. ... scribed families with a unique abundant element and again conjectured that\nThese observations are due to Poonen, who also found exceptions to t